A pattern may name a type the file cannot see, and nothing refuses it.

m/m.kso declares a private type `held` and a pub `make` that builds one.
u/u.kso imports nothing and writes an arm `shown (held v)`. A call `held 1`
in u would be error[name] (unknown name `held`). The pattern is not refused.

  kanso check .          ok
  kanso run . --interp   got nothing        (the arm never matches)
  kanso run .            error: native backend: unknown type `held`

Expected: error[name] at the pattern on every engine, the refusal a call to
the same name gets. Today the engines disagree, and native's message is an
internal one with no file or line.

The same shape in a *_test.kso (a test is an outside consumer, gavel
2026-10-12 on #1883) checks clean and makes the test fail at run time:
the mal port's `outcome_text (finished v w)` arm stopped matching when
`finished` was private, and kanso test reported "no overload of
`outcome_text` matches these arguments" rather than an unknown name.
